  "generalizedRange": "Upper Midwest and eastern United States",
  "hook": "A garden can help save this one. Really — yours.",
  "story": [
    {
      "id": "bee-look",
      "text": "The rusty patched bumble bee is a half-inch of fur and work: black head, yellow thorax, and on workers and males, a small rust-colored patch.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-pocket-guide", "wi-dnr-nest-story"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-nest",
      "text": "The whole colony lives underground, usually in an abandoned rodent burrow.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-nesting-overwintering"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-buzz",
      "text": "It works flowers the hard way: gripping a bloom in its jaws and vibrating its flight muscles until pollen shakes loose — the buzz that tomatoes and cranberries depend on.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-bumble-bees"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-year",
      "text": "Each colony lives one year. Every spring, a single overwintered queen rebuilds the whole society from scratch.",
      "sourceIDs": ["mn-dnr-state-symbols"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-collapse",
      "text": "Once common across twenty-eight states, it has vanished from an estimated 87 percent of its range.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-species-profile"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-listing",
      "text": "In January 2017 it became the first bee in the continental United States protected under the Endangered Species Act.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-first-bee-blog"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-causes",
      "text": "The suspects are quiet ones: disease spilling over from commercially raised bees, and neonicotinoid insecticides on the landscapes that remain.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-first-bee-blog", "xerces-species-profile"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-garden",
      "text": "This is a species you can help at home: native flowers blooming spring through fall, no pesticides, and leaves left lying in autumn — they are housing, not trash.",
      "sourceIDs": ["mn-dnr-pollinators", "xerces-leave-the-leaves"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-watch",
      "text": "Anyone with a camera can help too: photograph a bumble bee, and an expert will verify it on Bumble Bee Watch.",
      "sourceIDs": ["bumble-bee-watch"]
    },
    {
      "id": "bee-witness",
      "text": "To witness the rusty patched bumble bee is to notice the half-inch workers holding whole meadows together.",
      "sourceIDs": ["xerces-bumble-bees"]
    }
  ],
